Bone Fracture Classification with Custom CNNsProject OverviewThis project implements a deep learning pipeline to classify 10 different types of bone fractures from X-ray images. It explores the design, training, and evaluation of two distinct CNN architectures: a 2-layer model and a 3-layer model. The goal is to compare how increasing model depth affects the classification accuracy of complex medical imaging data.Project Structure1. Data PreparationDataset Management: Implements a custom MyDataset class to handle image loading and label mapping.Preprocessing: Images are resized to $224 \times 224$ pixels and normalized using specific mean and standard deviation values.Splitting: The data is divided into Training, Validation, and Test sets to ensure unbiased performance evaluation.2. Model ArchitectureThe core is a flexible MyCNN class that dynamically builds blocks based on input parameters:Convolutional Layers: Uses $3 \times 3$ kernels with ReLU activation.Pooling: $2 \times 2$ Max Pooling layers reduce spatial dimensions.Regularization: Dropout (0.5) is applied to prevent overfitting.Fully Connected Layer: Dynamically calculates input features based on the number of pooling layers ($56 \times 56$ for 2 layers; $28 \times 28$ for 3 layers).3. Training & Evaluation PipelineEpochs: Both models are trained for 50 epochs.Optimization: Uses the Adam optimizer ($LR=1e-3, Weight Decay=1e-4$) and Cross-Entropy Loss.Best Model Saving: The pipeline tracks validation accuracy and saves the "best" model weights to Assignment1_best_model.pt.Key Tasks & VisualizationsTask 6: Learning CurvesVisualizes training/validation loss and accuracy to monitor convergence.Provides insight into whether the model is overfitting or requires more training time.Task 7: Visual PredictionsDisplays test images with titles showing:GT: Ground Truth label.Pred: Predicted class index.P: Confidence probability (Softmax output).Task 8: Confusion MatrixA statistical breakdown of classification performance across all 10 classes.Identifies which specific fracture types the model confuses (e.g., Spiral vs. Oblique).Comparison: 2-Layer vs. 3-Layer ModelThe project concludes with a comparison of the two architectures:Model 1 (2 Layers): Uses layers_list = [32, 64].Model 2 (3 Layers): Uses layers_list = [16, 32, 64] to extract deeper hierarchical features.How to RunEnvironment: Ensure torch, torchvision, matplotlib, and sklearn are installed.Dataset: Place X-ray images in the directory structure expected by the data_path variable.Execution: Run the notebook cells sequentially. The training loop will automatically handle the 50 epochs and save the best weights.